%I A400942 #33 Nov 22 2026 20:10:20
%S A400942 0,1,2,1,2,2,1,2,2,2,1,2,2,2,3,1,2,2,2,2,3,1,2,2,2,3,3,3,1,2,2,2,3,3,
%T A400942 3,3
%N A400942 Triangle read by rows: T(n,k) is the minimum number of clues for a Numbrix puzzle with a unique solution on an n X k grid, 1 <= k <= n.
%C A400942 In an n X k Numbrix puzzle, you write the numbers 1, 2, ..., n*k in the cells so that consecutive numbers are always in adjacent cells, horizontally or vertically.
%C A400942 A clue is a number already written in a cell. A set of clues defines the puzzle if there is only one solution that contains them.
%C A400942 The minimum is taken across all puzzles: T(n,k) = m means that there exists at least one puzzle with m clues and a unique solution, and no such puzzle with fewer clues.
%C A400942 T(1,1) = 0, because a 1 X 1 grid can be filled in only one way.
%C A400942 Columns up to k=4 have formulas proven by Hanson and Nash.
%C A400942 Hanson and Nash conjectured that the minimum is 3 for 5 X n grids and 4 for 7 X n grids. T(6,5) = 2 shows that the first conjecture is false for n = 6. T(7,7) = T(8,7) = 3, on the other hand, show that the second conjecture is false for n = 7 and n = 8.
%H A400942 Mary Grace Hanson and David A. Nash, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.09389">Minimal and maximal Numbrix puzzles</a>, arXiv:1706.09389 [math.CO], 2017.
%H A400942 Emanuele Rizzan, <a href="https://github.com/clavis-systems/numbrix-minimum-clues">numbrix-minimum-clues</a>, GitHub, 2026.
%F A400942 T(n,k) <= ceiling(k/2) for 3 <= k <= n (Hanson and Nash, Theorem 8).
%F A400942 T(n,1) = 1 for n >= 2; T(n,2) = T(n,3) = T(n,4) = 2 for n >= 2 (Hanson and Nash, Theorems 5, 6, 10 and Corollary 9).
%e A400942 Triangle begins:
